+
+# SPEC: parse_duration input contract
+
+## 🎯 Intent
+`parse_duration` in `timeparse/parse.py` leans on `str.strip()`, `\d` and unbounded `int`, so it accepts Unicode whitespace and non-ASCII digits and has no upper bound. The user supplied an exhaustive input contract; this SPEC pins it so behavior no longer depends on Python's Unicode defaults.
+
+## 🌅 Outcomes
+Every input class below is accepted or rejected exactly as listed. Any input outside these classes behaves as it does today.
+
+## 📋 In-Scope Scenarios
+
+### S1: ASCII whitespace around the text is ignored
+**Given** a duration wrapped in spaces, tabs or newlines
+**When** `parse_duration(" 1h\t")` or `parse_duration("\n45s\n")` is called
+**Then** it returns 3600 and 45 respectively
+
+### S2: Other whitespace is rejected
+**Given** a duration with a non-ASCII-whitespace character around it
+**When** `parse_duration(" 1h")` or `parse_duration("1h ")` is called
+**Then** `ValueError` is raised
+
+### S3: Signs are rejected
+**Given** a duration containing `+` or `-`
+**When** `parse_duration("+1h")`, `"-1h"` or `"1h-30m"` is called
+**Then** `ValueError` is raised
+
+### S4: Only ASCII digits count
+**Given** a duration using Arabic-Indic or fullwidth digits
+**When** `parse_duration("١h")` or `parse_duration("１h")` is called
+**Then** `ValueError` is raised
+**And** `parse_duration("007s")` returns 7 (leading zeros allowed)
+
+### S5: Total is capped at 2**63 - 1 seconds
+**Given** a duration whose total is at or just over 9223372036854775807 seconds
+**When** `parse_duration("9223372036854775807s")` is called
+**Then** it returns 9223372036854775807
+**And** `"9223372036854775808s"`, `"106751991167301d"` and `"106751991167300d16h"` raise `ValueError`
+
+### S6: Empty or all-whitespace input is rejected
+**Given** `""` or `" \t\n"`
+**When** `parse_duration` is called
+**Then** `ValueError` is raised
+
+## 🚫 Non-Goals
+- No change to the grammar (unit order, no repeats, case-insensitive units) or to `format_duration` / the CLI beyond what flows through `parse_duration`.
+- No new exception type, no changes to error message wording.
+- No behavior change for inputs outside the listed classes.
+
+## ⚠️ Constraints
+| Constraint | Value | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|
+| Test framework | `pytest` | Already used in `tests/test_parse.py` (parametrized) |
+| Compatibility | Python as in `pyproject.toml` | No new dependency |
+| Scope | `timeparse/parse.py` only | Contract lives in one function |
+
+## 🔒 Irreversible Decisions
+none
+
+## ✅ Verification Criteria
+| Scenario | Verification mode | Test name / manual step |
+|---|---|---|
+| S1 | unit | `test_s1_ascii_whitespace_ignored` |
+| S2 | unit | `test_s2_other_whitespace_rejected` |
+| S3 | unit | `test_s3_signs_rejected` |
+| S4 | unit | `test_s4_ascii_digits_only`, `test_s4_leading_zeros_allowed` |
+| S5 | unit | `test_s5_int64_cap_accepts_max`, `test_s5_int64_cap_accepts_just_under`, `test_s5_int64_cap_rejects_over` |
+| S6 | unit | `test_s6_empty_rejected` |
+
+## ❓ Open Questions
+none
+
+## 🔍 Refinement Decisions
+- Interview skipped: the user's input contract is exhaustive and examples are given as goldens, so no WHAT ambiguity remains (autopilot `auto_full`, no question asked).
+- Lane `light`: one file, one session. `irreversible_decisions: []` — the change tightens accepted input of an existing function; revertible by a single commit and it adds no format, dependency or boundary.
+- Oracle: every AC is `golden`, with expected values taken verbatim from the user's contract (independent of the implementation).
+- spec-validator not dispatched: light lane, empty irreversible list.
